    "title": "Alabama lawmakers push solar farm ban bills as Stockton project raises concerns - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Kathryn Bruch ,\n1 hours ago\nSTOCKTON, Ala. ( WKRG ) — Time is running out to pass a bill to stop a solar farm in Baldwin County, as the legislative session is set to end early next month.\nMobile County woman charged with murder after 14-year-old autistic son’s death: new details from court testimony State Representative Matt Simpson is a local lawmaker who is trying to make that happen.\n“Unfortunately, it’s just popped up, and we didn’t have a ton of time through the legislative session,” Simpson said.\nSimpson’s bill, HB617 and HB618 , is headed to committee and focuses on concerns tied to solar developments. It would either add local oversight or potentially freeze projects for up to a year while guidelines are put in place.\nDuring a public hearing for those bills on Tuesday, Simpson told News 5 that lawmakers discussed concerns on both sides of proposed solar farm legislation, including the environmental impact and how quickly the developments are moving in areas like Stockton.\n“The concerns that were expressed were, you know, they didn’t want to interfere with other projects that are going on throughout the state,” Simpson said. “You know, solar and the power groups are very active, and trying to promote solar panels and promote these projects that they’re working on.”\nThe push comes after Silicon Ranch faced backlash over a proposed solar farm in Stockton that could be built on about 4,500 acres around nearby waterways.\n“The environmental impacts of this could be significant,” Simpson said. “Because that Stockton area touches the Tensaw River, it goes into the Mobile River, goes into potentially the bay and the Gulf itself.”\nSimpson said he is working alongside Alabama Sen. Greg Albritton as companion bills move through both the House and Senate.\n“We’ve got to make sure that we protect our area. We’ve got to protect our natural habitats for our children, for our children’s children,” he said.\nCourt documents reveal more about Prichard police officer accused of stealing guns, shooting roommate “It’s our responsibility to protect that quality of life.”\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WKRG News 5.",
    "summary": "STOCKTON, Ala. ( WKRG ) — Time is running out to pass a bill to stop a solar farm in Baldwin County, as the legislative session is set to end early next month. Mobile County woman charged with murder after 14-year-old autistic son’s death: new details from court testimony State Representative Matt Simpson is a local lawmaker who is trying to make that happen. It would either add local oversight or potentially freeze projects for up to a year while guidelines are put in place.",
